# The network of "Steering traffic and cloud circuits": a company's router
# with two private circuits to one cloud.
#
#   office 10.1.0.10 -- dc -- edge (AS 65001) ==dx1 169.254.30.0/30== cloud (AS 64600) -- vpc -- app 10.100.0.10
#                                            \==dx2 169.254.20.0/30==/
#
# dx1 and dx2 stand in for two dedicated circuits, such as the two links of
# an Azure ExpressRoute circuit: each is a cable with a BGP session over it.
# The cloud's router is scenery, configured and saved, and follows a
# provider's documented rules (the prompt quotes them); edge is the
# learner's. dx2's addresses are the lower ones, so when everything else
# about two paths is equal, the tie goes to dx2 on both routers (FRR also
# shares the traffic over two equal paths): a lab that asks for dx1 can't
# be passed by leaving things as they are. Each router's interfaces are
# named after the cable or network at the far end. Addresses come from the
# private ranges and the link-local block for circuits (169.254.0.0/16), as
# the clouds use, and AS numbers from the private range (RFC 6996).
machine office
machine app
machine edge router
machine cloud router

lan dc office:eth0=10.1.0.10/24 edge:lan=10.1.0.1/24
link edge:dx1=169.254.30.1/30 cloud:dx1=169.254.30.2/30
link edge:dx2=169.254.20.1/30 cloud:dx2=169.254.20.2/30
lan vpc cloud:vpc=10.100.0.1/16 app:eth0=10.100.0.10/16

route office default via 10.1.0.1
route app default via 10.100.0.1

run edge frr
run cloud frr
run office web-svc
run app web-svc
